'''bannock-fluke''' (ban′ọk-flök), ''n.'' [Sc., < ''bannock'' + ''fluke''<sup>2</sup>.] A Scotch name of the common turbot.

'''banns''' (banz), ''n. pl.'' [Formerly ''bannes'', often ''banes'', mod. more correctly ''bans'', pl. of ''ban''<sup>1</sup>, q. v. The spelling ''banns'' is now usual in this sense.] '''1.''' The proclamation of intended marriage in order that those who know of any impediment thereto may state it to the proper authorities. {{smaller|Banns were made a part of ecclesiastical legislation by the fourth Council of the Lateran, {{sc|a. d.}} 1215, whose decrees were confirmed by the Council of Trent. In the Roman Catholic Church the celebration of marriage without previous proclamation of the banns, unless by special dispensation, is gravely illicit, but not invalid. The proclamation is made by the parish priest of each contracting party, on three consecutive festivals during public mass. The proclamation of banns is no longer required in order to a valid civil marriage in England, Scotland, or the United States.}}
'''2†.''' The proclamation or prologue of a play.
{{smaller block|''Banes'' or Prologue [to] the Fall of Lucifer.
{{right|''York Plays'', Int., p. lxii.}}}}
{{smaller|'''To bid''' or '''ask the banns†''', to publish the banns.}}
{{smaller block|If all parties be pleased, ''ask'' their ''banns'', ’tis a match.
{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 561.}}}}
{{smaller|'''To forbid the banns''', to make formal objection to an intended marriage.}}
{{smaller block|A better fate did Maria deserve than to have her ''banns forbid.''
{{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, ix. 24.}}}}

'''bannut''' (ban′ut), ''n.'' [E. dial., also written ''bannet'', < late ME. ''bannenote'' (in comp. ''bannenotetre'', glossed ''avetana'', filbert); < *''ban'', *''banne''

readings.c1s3:
(origin unknown) + ''nut.''] A walnut, the fruit of ''Juglans regia'' (bannut-tree). [Obsolete or dialectal.]

'''banquet''' (bang′kwet), ''n.'' [Earlier ''banket'', < F. ''banquet'' (= It. ''banchetto'' = Sp. ''banquete''), a feast, orig. a little bench or table, dim. of ''banc'' (= It. Sp. ''banco''), a bench or table: see ''bank''<sup>1</sup> and ''banquette''.] '''1.''' A feast; a rich entertainment of food and drink.
{{smaller block|A napkin of fine linen to be laid on the table at the coronation ''banquet''.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xxiii.}}}}
'''2†.''' A light entertainment at the end of a feast; a dessert; a refection at which wine is drunk.
{{smaller block|<poem>We’ll dine in the great room; but let the music
And ''banquet'' be prepared here.</poem>
{{right|''Massinger'', Unnatural Combat, iii. 1.}}
There were all the dainties, not only of the season, but of what art could add, venison, plain solid meate, fowle, bak’d and boil’d meats, ''banquet'' (desert) in exceeding plenty, and exquisitely dress’d.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Oct. 27, 1685.}}}}
'''3†.''' A slight repast between meals: sometimes called ''running banquet''. ''N. E. D.''
{{smaller block|The ''running banquet'' of two beadles.
{{right|''Shak.'', Henry VIII., v. 3.}}}}
'''4†.''' In ''fort.'', same as ''banquette'', 1.—'''5.''' A small rod-shaped part of a horse’s bridle coming under the eye. ='''Syn. 1.''' {{smaller|''Feast'', ''Festival'', etc. See ''feast''.}}

'''banquet''' (bang′kwet), ''v.'' [Earlier ''banket'', < F. ''banqueter''; from the noun.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To treat with a feast or rich entertainment.
{{smaller block|<poem>You exceed in entertainment;
''Banquet'' our eyes too? ''Shirley'', The Traitor, iii. 2.</poem>}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|The illustrious company assembled there. {{right|''Coleridge.''}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To feast; regale one’s self with good eating and drinking; fare daintily.
{{smaller block|The mind shall ''banquet'', though the body pine.
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., i. 1.}}
<poem>Were it a draught for Juno when she ''banquets'',
I would not taste thy treasonous offer.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Comus, l. 701.}}}}
'''2†.''' To take part in a light refection after a feast. See ''banquet'', ''n.'', 2.
{{smaller block|Then was the banqueting-chamber in the tilt-yard at Greenwich furnished for the entertainment of these strangers, where they did both sup and ''banquet''.
{{right|''G. Cavendish.''}}}}

'''banquetant†''' (bang′kwet-ạnt), ''n.'' [< F. ''banquetant'', ppr. of ''banqueter'': see ''banquet'', ''v.''] One who banquets; a banqueter.
{{smaller block|<poem>Are there not beside
Other great ''banquetants''?</poem>
{{right|''Chapman'', Odyssey, xx.}}}}

'''banqueter''' (bang′kwet-ėr), ''n.'' '''1.''' A guest at a banquet; a feaster.
{{smaller block|Great ''banqueters'' do seldom great exploits. {{right|''Cotgrave.''}}}}
'''2†.''' One who provides feasts or rich entertainments.

'''banquet-hall''' (bang′kwet-hâl), ''n.'' A hall in which banquets are held. Also called ''banqueting-hall.''
{{smaller block|The fair Peleïan ''banquet-hall.'' {{right|''Tennyson'', Œnone.}}}}

'''banquet-house''' (bang′kwet-hous), ''n.'' A banqueting-house.
{{smaller block|A ''banquet-house'' salutes the southern sky. {{right|''Dryden.''}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''banqueting''' (bang′kwet-ing), ''n.'' The act of feasting; luxurious living; rich entertainment; a feast.
{{smaller block|Excess of wine, revellings, ''banquetings.'' {{right|1 Pet. iv. 3.}}}}

'''banqueting-hall''' (bang′kwet-ing-hâl), ''n.'' Same as ''banquet-hall.''

'''banqueting-house''' (bang′kwet-ing-hous), ''n.'' A house where banquets are given.
{{smaller block|In a ''banqueting-house'', among certain pleasant trees, the table was set. {{right|''Sidney.''}}}}

'''banquette''' (bong-ket′), ''n.'' [F., fem. dim. of ''banc'', a bench: see ''bank''<sup>2</sup>, and cf. ''banquet.''] '''1.''' (''a'') In ''fort.'', a raised way or foot-bank, running along the inside of a parapet breast-high above it, on which riflemen stand to fire upon the enemy. (''b'') In ''medieval fort.'', an advanced earthwork or palisaded defense outside of the ditch. {{smaller|The space between the ditch and the parapet was wide enough for a line of soldiers, but too narrow to allow of its being fortified if occupied by the besiegers. Formerly sometimes written ''banquet'', as English.}} '''2.''' The footway of a bridge when raised above the carriageway.—'''3.''' A bench for passengers, or the space occupied by benches, on the top of a French diligence, and hence of any public vehicle.—'''4.''' A sidewalk. [Common in the southern and southwestern United States.]
{{smaller block|Standing outside on the ''banquette'', he bowed — not to Dr. Mossy, but to the balcony of the big red-brick front. {{right|''G. W. Cable'', Old Creole Days, p. 144.}}}}
{{smaller block|'''Banquette slope''', in ''fort.'', an incline connecting the banquette tread with the terreplein or interior of the work. —'''Banquette tread''', the level surface of the banquette on which the soldiers stand while firing over the crest of the parapet; the tread.}}

